# I fine-tuned NVIDIA's Nemotron 3 Nano on harmless enterprise data. Then I re-ran the safety tests.

*Safety evidence has to travel with the weights. Here is a small, reproducible experiment showing why, and what an evidence package that survives fine-tuning could look like.*

---

Regulated enterprises are starting to adopt open-weight models. NVIDIA's Nemotron family is a natural candidate: permissive license, published training data, strong efficiency. But almost no enterprise deploys the model NVIDIA shipped. They fine-tune it on their own data, merge adapters, quantize, and increasingly blend it with other open models.

Every one of those steps produces new weights. The safety evaluations in the model card describe the old ones.

That gap matters. A 2023 study by Qi et al. ([Fine-tuning Aligned Language Models Compromises Safety, Even When Users Do Not Intend To](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.03693)) showed that fine-tuning on entirely benign data can erode a model's refusals. If that holds for the models enterprises are adopting now, then a model risk team that approved the base model has not approved what is actually running.

So I tested it myself, end to end, on a single GPU.

## The experiment

**Model.** Nemotron 3 Nano (30B total parameters, ~3B active; hybrid Mamba-Transformer mixture-of-experts), reasoning mode off, greedy decoding.

**Fine-tune.** A light LoRA (rank {{lora_r}}, {{epochs}} epochs) on {{train_n}} synthetic, programmatically generated master-data-management tasks: walking cost-center hierarchies, normalizing supplier records, entity matching, data-quality rule checks, and governed SQL. Nothing in the data is harmful. It is exactly the kind of terse, task-focused tuning data an enterprise data team would use.

**Three arms.**
1. **base**: the model as shipped.
2. **ft_domain**: base + LoRA on domain data only.
3. **ft_domain_replay**: same, but 10% of training rows are the base model's *own* refusals to harmful prompts, verified safe by the guard model. No new refusal text is written; the fine-tune just keeps rehearsing behavior the model already had.

**Measurement**, identical for every arm:
- **[garak](https://github.com/NVIDIA/garak)** red-team probes ({{garak_probes}}; {{garak_generations}} generations per prompt).
- **Llama-3.1-Nemotron-Safety-Guard-8B-v3** judging the model's answers to {{n_harmful}} harmful prompts from NVIDIA's Aegis 2.0 test set.
- **XSTest** ({{n_xstest_safe}} safe prompts that merely *sound* dangerous) to catch over-refusal, because a model that refuses everything is safe and useless.
- **Held-out domain accuracy** ({{n_domain}} tasks) to confirm the fine-tune actually taught something.

Every arm sees byte-identical prompts, so before/after differences are paired and attributable to the weights.

